    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the Western Pacific Railroad in 1909–1910 turned Oasis into a key siding, shipping cattle and hay to distant markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oasis_nevada"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Oasis sits at about 5,870 feet, nestled between the towering Pequop Mountains and the Toano Range.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oasis%2C_Nevada"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Before settlers, the Goshute people used the valley’s springs and wetlands for hunting and gathering.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oasis_nevada"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By the 1870s, ranchers began grazing cattle and sheep on the valley’s grasslands, marking the shift from transient use to permanent settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/oasis_nevada"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Oasis lies at the junction of Interstate 80 and Nevada State Route 233, a crossroads that has guided travelers across the high desert.",
